# Line endings for OUR source: LF in the repo and LF on disk, every platform.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. This repo is written on Windows and built on Linux CI. With
# Git's Windows default (`core.autocrlf=true`) a file is stored as LF but
# checked out as CRLF, so the same file is a different sequence of bytes
# depending on which machine reads it. That has already cost us twice:
#
#   1. A migration guard split files on "\n" only. On a Windows checkout every
#      line ends "\r\n", and JavaScript's `.` cannot match a carriage return —
#      so `/--.*$/` stripped nothing and the guard read a SQL comment as
#      destructive DDL. It accused another agent's correct migration. CI was
#      green the whole time, because CI checks out LF. The bug existed on one
#      machine only, which is the hardest kind to believe.
#
#   2. Any tool that rewrites a tracked file — npm touching package-lock.json,
#      a formatter, a codemod — writes CRLF, and git then reports the file as
#      modified with no content change. That noise hides real diffs and invites
#      someone to `git checkout --` over work they meant to keep.
#
# SCOPE IS DELIBERATELY NARROW. An earlier draft of this file used a blanket
# `* text=auto eol=lf`. Measuring before committing showed it would also have
# rewritten `android/gradlew.bat` — and a Windows batch file with LF endings
# can be misparsed by cmd.exe. The `android/` tree is Capacitor-generated, it
# is not where either bug happened, and an Android build cannot be verified
# from this machine. So this file governs the source we actually write and
# leaves everything else exactly as git handled it before.
#
# Verified before committing: of 400 tracked source files, ZERO were stored
# with CRLF — so this changes no repository content. It only stops Windows
# checkouts from rewriting them on disk.

*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.jsx   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# NOTE: *.bat / *.cmd / *.ps1 are deliberately NOT listed. Windows batch files
# need CRLF, and every way I tried to express that here left gradlew.bat
# showing as modified. Since android/ is Capacitor-generated, is not where
# either bug happened, and an Android build cannot be verified from this
# machine, the right move was to take it out of scope entirely rather than
# ship a rule I could not prove. If shell scripts under android/ ever need
# normalising, that is a separate, testable change.

# Binary — never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.xls  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks  binary
